# Lesson 3, new exercise: count four losers; draw trumps, then ruff two diamonds in dummy.
title: Dummy's short diamonds
id: L03-13
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.3
student: S
board: 45
dealer: N
vulnerable: both

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  K863   A43    K7     Q853
  E  54     QJ52   JT95   T97
  S  AQJ72  K8     A842   62
  W  T9     T976   Q63    AKJ4

auction:
  N 1C     = 13+ points, at least four clubs
  E Pass
  S 1S*    = 6+ points, at least four spades
     not *: With 6+ points and a 4-card major, what does the lesson respond?
  W Pass
  N 2S     = Four spades, a minimum opening
  E Pass
  S 4S*    = Game
     key: 16 points with your doubletons, and partner opened: at least 13. That's 29. Bid game.
     not Pass: Did you add it up? Partner opened, so 16 plus 13 is well past 25.
     not 3S: Can your side have less than 25? Partner opened, so you have at least 29.
     not *: Partner opened and has four spades. Can you see 25 points?
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  Partner opened 1♣ and raised your spades. With 16 points opposite an
  opening bid, you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid spades first,
  and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 2
  spades: 0 = Solid.
  hearts: 0 = The ace and king are both there.
  diamonds: 0 = Two small ones after the ace and king, but dummy can ruff both.
  clubs: 2 = The ace and king.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand.
  remind: Dummy has only two diamonds. What can it do on the third and fourth rounds?
play:
  1   W CA  N C3  E C7  S C2
      note: Four losers: two clubs and two diamonds. The defense is taking the clubs now. Dummy's short diamonds can take care of the other two.
  2   W CK  N C5  E C9  S C6
  3   W S9  N S3  E S4  S SJ
      note: West switches to a trump. Win, and draw the rest.
  4   S SA  W ST  N S6  E S5
      note: Both opponents followed twice. Trumps are drawn, and dummy still has two.
  5   S D2  W D3  N DK  E D5
      note: Now the diamonds: the king, then the ace.
  6   N D7  E D9  S DA  W D6
      note: Over to your ♦A.
  7   S D4  W DQ  N S8  E DT
      note: Dummy is out of diamonds. Ruff.
  8   N H3  E H2  S HK  W H6
      note: Back to your hand with the ♥K...
  9   S D8  W H7  N SK  E DJ
      note: ...and ruff your last diamond.
  10  N HA  E H5  S H8  W H9
      note: Cash the ♥A.
  11  N CQ  E CT  S S2  W C4
      note: Dummy's ♣Q is high now. You have no clubs, so you must ruff it, but that's fine: the rest are yours.
  12  S SQ  W CJ  N H4  E HJ
      note: Your last trumps are winners.
  13  S S7  W HT  N C8  E HQ
      note: 11 tricks: 4♠ made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: draw trumps, then use dummy's short diamonds and spare trumps to
  ruff your two diamond losers.

  You lost only the two clubs. Dummy's two small trumps each won a trick,
  which they could never have done if you had played the hand at no-trump.
